        'Displacement current is the current equivalent of a time-varying electric flux, I_d = ε₀(dΦ_E/dt). SI unit: ampere (A). Ampere–Maxwell law: ∮B·dl = μ₀(I_c + ε₀ dΦ_E/dt).',
      answerType: 'text',
      hint: 'Recall I_d = ε₀(dΦ_E/dt); the modified Ampere law adds the displacement term to the conduction current.',
    },
    {
      number: 2,
      bloomLevel: 'apply',
      question:
        'A parallel-plate capacitor of area A = 0.02 m² is charged by a steady current I = 5 mA. Show that the displacement current between the plates equals the conduction current, and find dE/dt (in V·m⁻¹·s⁻¹, scientific notation like 2.82e10).',
      answer: '2.82e10',
      answerType: 'text',
      unit: 'V·m⁻¹·s⁻¹',
      formula: 'dE/dt = I/(ε₀A)',
      working:
        'I_d = ε₀(dΦ_E/dt) = ε₀·(I/ε₀) = I = 5 mA. dE/dt = I/(ε₀A) = 5e-3/(8.854e-12 × 0.02) ≈ 2.82×10¹⁰ V·m⁻¹·s⁻¹.',
      hint: 'Use E = q/(ε₀A), so dE/dt = (dq/dt)/(ε₀A) = I/(ε₀A).',
    },
    {
